package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Start child agent sessions and work with them: send them input, wait for
/// their results, cancel them, end them, and see the tree they form.
/// Unstable behind `agent-spawn`.
///
/// A child is an agent session of its own: an agent component (targeting
/// `runnable-agent`) in its own workload, with its own conversation, grants,
/// toolsets and session state, inheriting nothing. Loading a toolset into
/// the caller's own workload is `tool-loading`, a separate power. Both share
/// `toolset-spec`, `admission-error` and the ceiling rules in `spawn-types`.
///
/// Tree. Every child session records its parent, so sessions form a tree
/// rooted at the session a person or trigger started. The caller lists its
/// descendants (state only) and acts only on its direct children, by
/// session id. A host reads the same tree from its session store to display
/// it, and routes a child's approvals to the root session's person,
/// labelled with the child's place in the tree.
///
/// Location. Every operation names a child by session id and works through
/// the host's session store, so a child may run on another host, host group
/// or zone, and may move between hosts between runs.
/// `spawn-request.placement` is a hint. A session id authorizes nothing on
/// its own: the host records the identity of the caller that spawned each
/// child (workload incarnation and component name; see caller identity in
/// `spawn-types`) and acts only for that caller, so a sibling component
/// neither sees nor drives another component's children. When that identity
/// ceases to exist (the workload is deleted or recreated), its children end.
///
/// Leases and fencing. A child's run holds an exclusive lease in the session
/// store, with a fencing generation: a counter the store advances when it
/// grants a lease and closes when the lease expires. The store must be
/// linearizable (compare-and-swap) for leases, generations, spawn tickets
/// and limit reservations; a store or zone that cannot be answers
/// `unavailable`.
///
/// - The store decides expiry: the operator's timeout, recommended 60
///   seconds after the last renewal. Expiry closes the generation at once,
///   so the store rejects any later write, outcome or tool dispatch it
///   mediates for that run. Whichever host next reads the child reports the
///   run as failed: waiters get `run-error.host-lost`, the child's state
///   becomes `failed`, and `child.failure` says why. No host has to be
///   alive for that.
/// - The running host fences itself: by its monotonic clock, once the time
///   since its last successful renewal passes the store's timeout minus a
///   margin (recommended 15 seconds), it stops the child's instances (the
///   agent and its toolsets), so nothing new starts through any of their
///   imports, and cancels what is in flight.
/// - Fencing does not undo or stop effects already under way (a request
///   sent, a write issued), and cannot help when the host's clock misbehaves
///   (a paused virtual machine resumes after its lease expired). Those may
///   overlap a new run, within the child's own reach and never more.
/// - Brokered calls carry the generation. Every call the host brokers for a
///   run (model calls, credential-brokered egress, tool dispatch, writes
///   through a host-managed store) carries the run's fencing generation. A
///   broker on the run's own host admits it only while that host's lease
///   view is valid (the same clock and margin as the self-fence). A broker
///   elsewhere, such as a shared model or credential gateway, checks the
///   generation with the store and fails closed when it cannot confirm the
///   generation is open. Direct egress through a component's own grants
///   cannot be fenced this way.
/// - Re-placement delay. After a lease expires, the child's next run is
///   accepted but does not start until the operator's re-placement delay
///   has passed (at least the self-fencing margin, recommended 30 seconds);
///   its progress shows `blocked(placement-delay)`.
///
/// The child's session is durable: a later `send` starts a new run, placed
/// again, under a new generation. After a run that did not finish, the
/// child's conversation holds every step the store recorded under that
/// run's generation, then a host-written marker saying how the run ended
/// (cancelled, failed, host lost, revoked). The next run resumes from there;
/// nothing is replayed.
///
/// Stopping. `cancel` cancels a child's run in progress and every run in
/// progress below it. `end` ends the child and every session below it. A
/// person's stop on a session in the host's UI cancels its subtree's runs
/// the same way. A run that ends any other way leaves its children's runs
/// going, bounded by the tree limits and budgets.
///
/// Cancellation is cooperative first. After the operator's grace period
/// (recommended 15 seconds), each host still running a run in the subtree
/// stops that run's instances as it would to fence itself, and metering
/// stops. A host the request cannot reach is stopped by its lease: the run
/// ends `host-lost`, and the host fences itself by its own deadline.
/// `cancel` and `end` resolve when every run in the subtree has stopped or
/// lost its lease, so within the longer of the grace period and the lease
/// timeout; dropping the future only stops waiting. A person's stop has the
/// same deadline. Effects already under way are not undone.
///
/// Cancellation is a barrier. `cancel`, `end` and a person's stop atomically
/// mark the subtree as cancelling in the session store before cancelling its
/// runs. Every `spawn` and `send` from a run in that subtree, including one
/// already in flight, is checked against the mark at acceptance and refused
/// (`denied` or `start-error.denied`). The mark lifts for a session when its
/// cancelled run has ended.
///
/// Lifetime and limits. A child lives until `end`, until its `max-duration`
/// passes, or until its parent session ends; ending a session ends its
/// descendants. A child with no run in progress holds no instance. Tree
/// limits are always set and operator-adjustable: `depth` (default 3),
/// `concurrent-agents` (runs from acceptance until they end, default 20),
/// `live-sessions` (child sessions not yet ended, default 50) and
/// `total-agents` (children spawned in all, default 500). Every root session
/// has a finite token and wall-clock budget, which an operator may raise but
/// never remove. Every spawn and every run acceptance (`send`, or `spawn`
/// with input) reserves its place atomically across the tree. Tokens a
/// child's subtree spends count against the child's `max-total-tokens` and
/// every ancestor's.
///
/// Attachments belong to their session. On `send` (and `spawn` with input)
/// the host copies each stored attachment the parent can read into the
/// child's session and re-issues it there. An outcome's stored attachments
/// are copied into the parent's session the same way and count toward
/// `output.max-bytes`; with `output.structured-only`, an outcome carries
/// none.
///
/// Notices. `watch` streams changes to the caller's children within a run.
/// Across runs, the host keeps a notice for each run the caller started in a
/// child when that run ends (session id, run id, how it ended; never child
/// text) until the caller acknowledges it, and `read-notices` returns them
/// in the caller's next run. Whether a child's completion starts the
/// parent's next run is a host trigger, outside this interface.
///
/// Traces. The implementer propagates the parent run's trace context
/// (`run-options.trace-parent`) to every child run it starts, on any host,
/// and records spawn, send, cancel, end, lease expiry and revocation as span
/// events, with the session id as the conversation id.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{user-message};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use session-status.{status};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{
        spawn-request, admission-error, start-error, run-error, started-run,
        budget-item,
    };
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};

    /// Why a child ended a run as failed, or cannot take another.
    /// Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-failure {
        /// The host running the child went away mid-run: its lease in the
        /// session store expired. The detail names the host for display.
        host-lost(string),
        /// The child's components could not start.
        start-failed(string),
        /// A budget item is spent, its own or an ancestor's; the child takes
        /// no more runs.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// The child's admission no longer fits its ancestors' current
        /// ceilings or policy (an ancestor lost a grant or a toolset, or a
        /// person removed one), so it takes no more runs.
        revoked(string),
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// One session in the caller's subtree, as the host's session store has
    /// it.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record child {
        /// The child's session id: host-minted, unique, stable for the
        /// session's life, and valid on any host that shares the session
        /// store.
        session: string,
        /// Its parent's session id: the caller's for a direct child.
        parent: string,
        /// Its depth, where a child of a session a person or trigger started
        /// is at depth 1.
        depth: u32,
        /// The request's labels: set for the caller's direct children, and
        /// empty for deeper descendants, whose labels a child wrote.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Its session state (`session-status`). None when the host could
        /// not read it.
        status: option<status>,
        /// Why its last run failed or why it takes no more runs, when one
        /// applies.
        failure: option<child-failure>,
        /// Where it last ran, as the host names it for display (a host,
        /// host group or zone). Display only; never an address.
        location: option<string>,
        /// What it and every session below it have spent, metered by the
        /// host, never reported by the child.
        usage: usage-total,
        /// True once the session has ended. Ended children stay listed for
        /// a bounded time and number (recommended the 100 most recent per
        /// tree).
        ended: bool,
    }

    /// Spend metered by the host for a session and its subtree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record usage-total {
        /// Prompt plus completion tokens.
        tokens: u64,
        /// Runs accepted.
        runs: u32,
        /// Model calls made.
        model-calls: u32,
    }

    /// What remains of a budget. Tokens and time are the tightest of the
    /// session's own and every ancestor's. None in a field means no limit
    /// applies to it below the root's.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record remaining-budget {
        /// Tokens left.
        tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Wall-clock time left.
        duration: option<duration>,
        /// Runs left, from this session's own `max-runs` only; runs are not
        /// charged to ancestors.
        runs: option<u32>,
    }

    /// The caller's own place in the tree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record lineage {
        /// The caller's session id.
        session: string,
        /// Its parent's session id; none for a root session.
        parent: option<string>,
        /// Its depth; 0 for a root session.
        depth: u32,
        /// What the caller has left to spend.
        remaining: remaining-budget,
    }

    /// A change to the caller's subtree, from `watch`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-event {
        /// A child as it stands when the watch starts. All of these come
        /// first.
        existing(child),
        /// The snapshot is complete; changes follow.
        synced,
        /// A child's state, failure, usage or ended flag changed. Carries
        /// the whole record as it stands now.
        changed(child),
        /// An event not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// How a run the caller started in a child ended. Host-authored; never
    /// child text.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-end {
        /// It finished with an outcome; `wait` returns it.
        done,
        /// It was cancelled.
        cancelled,
        /// It failed; `wait` returns the error.
        failed,
        /// Its host was lost.
        host-lost,
        /// An ending not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A notice that a run the caller started in a child has ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice {
        /// Its place in the caller's notice sequence, increasing by one.
        seq: u64,
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The run's id, as `wait` takes it.
        run: string,
        /// How it ended.
        ended: run-end,
    }

    /// Why an operation on a child did nothing.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-error {
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller.
        unknown,
        /// The child's session has ended.
        ended,
        /// The session store could not be reached, or could not answer
        /// consistently. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why `wait` returned no outcome.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant wait-error {
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller, or the
        /// session store could not be reached.
        child(child-error),
        /// No run with that id was started by the caller in that child, or
        /// its outcome is no longer kept.
        unknown-run,
        /// The run failed.
        run(run-error),
    }

    /// A run the caller started in a child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record sent {
        /// The run's id, minted by the host: what `wait` takes.
        run: string,
        /// Progress, then the outcome.
        started: started-run,
    }

    /// Notices from `read-notices`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice-batch {
        /// The notices, oldest first.
        notices: list<notice>,
        /// True when the host dropped unacknowledged notices older than
        /// these, past its retention.
        gap: bool,
    }

    /// What `spawn` started.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawned {
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The first run, when `input` was given.
        run: option<sent>,
    }

    /// Check the request against the ceiling, policy and the tree limits,
    /// and start a child session, plus its first run when `input` is given.
    /// Resolves once accepted, not once the child is running; a child that
    /// fails to start reports it on its run, its state and `failure`.
    ///
    /// `key` is the caller's idempotency key: any string up to 128 bytes,
    /// unique per intended child within the caller's session. For the life
    /// of the caller's session, a resend with a key that already admitted a
    /// child returns that child, whatever else it says, and starts no second
    /// first run; a different key is a different child. Key records are
    /// bounded by `total-agents`. When requests with different keys share
    /// one pending approval, its grant admits the first key to resend and
    /// the others get a new approval marked as a repeat, so one decision
    /// admits one child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    spawn: async func(
        request: spawn-request,
        input: option<user-message>,
        key: string,
    ) -> result<spawned, admission-error>;

    /// Start a run in a direct child: hand it a message and let it work
    /// until it replies or a budget item stops it. Resolves when the run is
    /// accepted. The host first re-checks the child's admission against
    /// every ancestor's current ceiling and policy (`denied`, with
    /// `child.failure` `revoked`, when it no longer fits). `busy` while a run
    /// is in progress there (the caller's, or one a person started by
    /// talking to the child), or a cancelled run has not stopped.
    ///
    /// `key` is an idempotency key, as for `spawn`: a resend with a key that
    /// already started a run in that child returns the same `sent` (the same
    /// run id, a fresh view of its progress and outcome) and starts nothing,
    /// whether that run is in progress or ended. The host keeps the keys of
    /// the 256 most recent runs per child; an older key starts a new run. A
    /// known key whose outcome is no longer kept still returns the same run,
    /// with its outcome resolving to `run-error.expired`. With none, every
    /// call starts a run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    send: async func(
        session: string,
        message: user-message,
        key: option<string>,
    ) -> result<sent, start-error>;

    /// The outcome of one run the caller started in a direct child, by its
    /// run id: once it ends, or at once if it has ended. Works from any
    /// later run and any host that shares the session store. The host keeps
    /// at least the 16 most recent such outcomes per child until the child's
    /// session ends.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    wait: async func(session: string, run: string) -> result<run-outcome, wait-error>;

    /// Cancel a direct child's run in progress and every run in progress
    /// below it: cooperatively, then by force after the host's grace period.
    /// Resolves when the whole subtree has stopped; each run ends
    /// `cancelled`. Nothing to cancel is ok.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    cancel: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// End a direct child's session and every session below it, cancelling
    /// runs in progress as `cancel` does. Resolves when the whole subtree
    /// has stopped. They take no more runs and stay listed as ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    end: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's children, oldest first, with their state. With
    /// `descendants`, every session below the caller, each listed after its
    /// parent. Bounded by the tree limits and the retention of ended
    /// children.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    children: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<list<child>, child-error>;

    /// The caller's children (or, with `descendants`, its whole subtree) as
    /// they stand, then each change as it happens. A reader that falls
    /// behind may miss an intermediate change but always sees each child's
    /// latest record. Dropping the stream stops watching; it ends when the
    /// caller's session ends.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    watch: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<stream<child-event>, child-error>;

    /// Unacknowledged notices of runs the caller started in its children,
    /// oldest first. Reading clears nothing, so a reply lost before the
    /// caller recorded it is read again. The host keeps at least the 256
    /// most recent unacknowledged notices per session; `gap` is true when
    /// older ones were dropped. A harness reads them at the start of each
    /// run and acknowledges what it has recorded.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    read-notices: async func() -> result<notice-batch, child-error>;

    /// Acknowledge every notice up to and including `through`; they are no
    /// longer returned. Acknowledging again is harmless.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    ack-notices: async func(through: u64) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's own session id, parent, depth and remaining budget.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    get-lineage: async func() -> result<lineage, child-error>;
}
